# AUTHOR REPORT — Book 4, Movement 1 "Last Term, First Sentence" (chapters 1–7)

Author: Monroe Jackson (seat `oconnor` 1.3.0, model `claude-opus-5-5`) · 2026-10-04 (one session; see AUTHORSHIP.md)

**Reader Standard.** `ed.sh gates book-04-copper-crown 1`: reader_standard=0, metadata=0, modern=0 on all seven chapters.
- No oaths or softer cousins anywhere, including the gallery's noise at the rematch.
- Violence with cost and no gore: forearms "humming" and aching from bursting shells, a light edge along the ribs, the hip line "like a seam somebody had stitched into him with hot wire", three days off the floor held to the hour.
- No romance. Lira's hand on his head "briefly and rather hard", her shoulder knock, the cold cloth; nothing more.
- Wrong named as wrong: Cael refuses the flattering log ("a lie by omission was still a lie"); he declines to want the clause alive before it is proved; he corrects the hatch woman's rumour kindly and without letting it stand; Withrow names her twenty years of filing sound law in drawers as a thing she was "good at" and will not do again.

