editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:19:It had started on the third day with a second-year in the corridor outside the north hall who had stopped talking as Cael passed and then started again a little louder, and it had gone on from there, because people here did not wonder what he was, the way they had at Greyvane; they told each other. Each of them had a version, and each version was finished, and no two of them were the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:31:Nearest the record stood one sentence: *made his argument in public, and the law turned out to say what he said it said.* That was so. As far as he could tell, it was held by the registrar and the chancellor and perhaps two others, all of whom had read the transcript.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:33:One mouth further out it became *beat the Compact in court.* That had not happened: a panel had found that the Compact's own schedule did not name him, and had dismissed a challenge for that reason and no other. But the two were close enough that putting the one right would only make him look as if he wanted to be thanked for being modest. He let it stand and hated letting it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:43:*At Greyvane the question of what I was had no answer, and so it stayed open. That was hard to live in, but an open door can still be walked through later, by somebody carrying proof.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:55:Lira had found out about it and saved him a place with her boot. He had expected the back rows to be empty, and instead there were sixty-odd students in them, some writing, most just watching, like people at the rail of a ring before the bout. Down on the floor of the theater the faculty sat round a long table: forty of them, with Chancellor Withrow at the centre and a clerk at her elbow keeping the minute book. The theater could hold two hundred and forty, and it felt full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:59:The item came up a little after the half-hour, and the clerk read its title off the agenda. *Enrollments resting on a provision: report of the registrar.* Bracken stood, spoke for less time than it took the sand in the clerk's glass to run, and sat. Nobody asked him anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:73:The pen started again. Withrow turned a page of her agenda. "Next, the bursar, on the glazing in the north hall." The bursar stood up and began to talk about cracked panes and the cost of lead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:79:Afterward, on the way out, Cael stopped at the board by the theater door where the clerk had already pinned the session's minute. He read down to the item.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:95:"I don't know." He looked at the minute again. "Neither does he. That's why he wanted the date on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:101:"Appeals," she said. "The chancellor writes one every year to the academy's old students and friends, asking for money. These are the last four. Read the second paragraph of each, left to right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:103:He read them. The first second paragraph talked about the school's place among its peers. The next talked about that place and added a hope. The third gave the hope a name. The fourth named it twice, once at the top of the paragraph and once again in the last line of the letter, in capitals.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:113:"Off the ladder. It's in the appendix to the charter, two floors down. I read it on Tuesday." Karis pulled the folded blanket off the chair, sat, and drew it round her shoulders, because the law range had no stove and it was cold at the top of the building. "Now look at the second sheet. Six years ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:115:The last line of the second appeal asked for gifts *toward the instruments by which a school is known.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:153:It was ruled down the middle. She had written in a broad square hand on both sides of the rule, and he read it standing in front of the case with the three crowns at his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:159:He read both columns twice and gave the sheet back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:169:"I read the hearing transcript four times. Not the summary — the transcript, all sessions, all exhibits. The law is on your side of it. I spent twenty years inside that registry. I know exactly how rare that is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:189:He read it in the cloister by the pigeonholes, and then read the last sentence again on its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:195:Karis had brought three books out of carrel eleven to read in the last of the light. Brom sat with his back against the coping, his new wraps across his knees, saying nothing about the east hall, though by now his silence about it had begun to be loud. Lira was leaning on the stone with her eyes on the ferry landing across the water, where two grey coats were standing under a lamp and two more were coming up the river street to take their places.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:199:He passed the notice along the wall. Lira read it and gave it to Brom. Brom read it slowly and gave it to Karis. Karis read it twice, folded it on its crease, and handed it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:219:He read that back. Then he wrote the rest, because a binder that kept only the comfortable half was no use to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:223:*The first kind of roof I can climb up and check. The second I can only live under. I have. It didn't fall in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:5:The question was not *what is this place*. Karis already had that out of the prospectus, and she would have the rest of it out of the library inside a week. His was older and sharper, and he had brought it down from Greyvane in the same pocket as the notebook. *Why did they take me?* Every school he had stood in told one story across a desk and another in its stone. The desk story was for visitors. The stone story was in which roof got mended first and which teacher got the good hall, in what was weighed every week and what was left to the damp. Anybody willing to walk slowly could read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:9:He walked the north edge first, where the three long halls stood with their high windows facing the morning. The largest had six floors marked out on its boards, and somebody was already on one of them at that hour, alone, running a drill he did not know. The second hall was smaller and its floors were timber, not stone, and when he stood in its doorway and shifted his weight he felt the boards answer him very slightly, a give and a return, as though the floor had a breath of its own. He wrote that down and underlined it and did not yet know why. The third hall was low and set apart behind the assessment wing. Its door was locked from the outside, with the key on a nail beside it, which was a thing he had never seen before and could not think of a reason for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:17:The first quadrangle was the oldest and had the best rooms, with deep windows and doors that shut properly. The third was newest and largest, built in a hurry, and its stair rang under every step. Theirs, the second, sat between. On the second morning he watched the porters carrying trunks across from the gate, and he noticed which trunks went where. It was not by tier, or not only by tier. A Copper girl whose tag carried a small painted mark went to the first quadrangle. Two Iron boys with plain tags and quiet faces went to the third. He asked the porter, idly, what the painted mark was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:25:Cael wrote that down in the notebook, and then sat on the steps of the second quadrangle for a long time looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:27:Lira found him there a little after the second bell. She had been up before him, which was usual, and she had already walked the whole of the Crown yard's rim once, slowly, and stood a long while at the registrar's table at its side, where the ladder list lay open for names. She had not written hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:37:He showed her the line about the rooms. She read it twice, and then gave it back to him without saying anything. That was how he knew it was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:47:It was in his own hand, from the evening before, in the middle of a page of others like it: *Current Path, tall, dark braid, third year by the tag. Favours the right leg coming down stairs. Old injury, healed short.* It was a good entry, and he remembered making it: the braid, the stair outside the north hall, and the small hitch in the right leg on the second step down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:59:A Force Path cohort was on the largest floor, twenty-two students and four instructors, running a rotation he had not learned. He sat on the gallery bench and did the thing he had been doing for three days without thinking about it. He ran the gaze across the floor, all four layers at once, rhythm and weight and breath and position, building each student as a live thing in his head and keeping them all moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:93:*Standing.* These were the people he would carry at full depth all the time, kept live and updated every day. Eight of them at the most, and never nine, because nine was where the failing started and he meant to keep one place clear between himself and the edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:111:"Yes," said Karis. "Everybody does that. You're only the first person I've met who's going to write the list down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:119:Karis leaned over and read the three names upside down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:141:It was dressed stone, sixty feet by forty, laid so close that he could not get a fingernail into the joints. And it was sunk. The whole floor lay four feet below the ground around it, with three short flights of steps going down into it. The tiered seats did not rise from the floor's edge the way the benches at Greyvane had risen, but from the ground's edge, from above. He stood in the middle of the stone floor and turned slowly round, and on three sides the seats went up and back from a point above his head, row on row, pale and empty, and every one of them was looking down at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:157:"Thought so. You read things." He wiped. "Go on, then. Read 'em. I'll tell you what you get wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:159:He read them and wrote them down as he went, in the shorthand he kept for things he would need again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:177:"How does it work?" he asked the porter. "The ladder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:187:*Sit out four sessions running and you slip one anyway. Year's end: the best four in each column are seeded to the semifinals, which run in the second term's closing weeks. The final comes on the year's last day of competition. Faculty sit as the panel. The registrar keeps the record.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:193:"The winner gets the crown," he said. "There's three. Copper, Iron, Silver. They live in a case in the chancellor's hall and I polish them, so I know." He started wiping again, slower. "The Copper's the eldest by a long way. They've been riveting new brass on under the old for a hundred and thirty-one years, to make room for the names. Two years now it's been Fiske's. Force Path, Copper Rank Eight. Put her in the Iron column tomorrow and she'd walk off with that one as well."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:203:They crossed the bridge on foot, and Brom paid, and said very little on the way over. He walked a little faster than usual, and twice Cael caught him turning his right hand over and looking at the back of it, the way he looked at a joint after a fall to see whether it had swollen, though he had not fallen and nothing on him was swollen. Cael watched him do it the second time and did not ask. Brom had a face on him that was not ready yet, and Brom always came out with things in his own time and never before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:205:On the river street, in the long light, they walked past what Cael had come down to see. Every second shop near the bridge foot sold the same things, wraps and soft-soled shoes and quilted jackets hung in the windows like washing, and nobody kept a row of shops like that alive on passing trade: somebody up the hill was buying all of it, every season, by the cartload. Along the river road the lodging houses had boards out by their doors, *Term lets. Rooms by the term. Academy families welcome.*, freshly painted over older paint that said the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:235:Two nights later, on the eleventh day, Cael wrote it down at the desk by his window, with the second quadrangle dark below and the lamps of Ostrand across the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:241:*A circuit that pays coin has to tell you the price before the bout. You know what you're fighting for, and so does everybody watching. A ladder that pays in standing never has to say. It just moves you up or down a wall, and lets you work out afterward what it cost.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:5:His hip had given him back on the fourth morning, as he had written that it would. He had kept Lira's three days to the hour. He had not taken a stair two at a time, though he had wanted to on the second evening, badly, and Lira had been on the landing above with her arms folded as if she had known. Now he was standing at the pump with the handle under one hand, not pumping, only feeling the iron cold under his palm and the old ease back in his leading side. He was thinking about nothing in particular, which was rare enough that he noticed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:19:She would never say she was proud of him, or that she wished it had been her. Quenna did not put into words anything she could not afterward stand behind on a record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:33:It was one line long, in the clerk's small even hand. It said that the enrollment of the candidate on the observer track concluded at the end of the present term by transfer to a chartered institution, at the candidate's request, as entered by the provost. It cited nothing. It explained nothing. Karis read it three times and pronounced it perfect.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:35:"Not one interesting word," she said. "He's done it again. That paper is going to sit in a drawer for a hundred years without anybody ever wanting to read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:39:"It's the best thing that can happen to a paper." She copied it into her narrow brown notebook, all of it, the date included. "The interesting ones get read by people with pens."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:43:He wrote to Hesk on the Tuesday night. It was a short letter, shorter than the last, because he had learned that Hesk read the short ones more than once. He said where they were going, and why, and that there was a registrar at the other end who had asked how to spell Hesk's name and had got it in capitals. He said the four of them were well. He did not say anything about the bout. He thought about it, and found what he wanted to write was *I won by one exchange and maybe none*, and he could not see a way to put that to Hesk that would not sound like asking to be told it was all right. He left it out. Hesk would hear it from somebody, or he would not, and either way it would keep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:47:The letter had gone down the hill at the end of the winter term, in his own hand, with the ruling copied out under it word for word. It had been five months. He had stopped listening for the coach somewhere around week six, and had started again around week fifteen, and had made himself stop a second time. Vell had never in her life answered anything quickly. She read a letter the way she read a ledger, every line in order, and then she put it somewhere and let it settle before she touched it again. He knew that. It did not make the silence lighter. It only told him what shape it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:107:He watched her go with the sheaf in his hands, and saw he had had the gap the wrong way round all term. Prynn was not failing to close it. She was keeping it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:115:"I'll copy it," said Karis. "On the road. Every evening. You can read it while I copy." She looked up the cart road after Prynn's black coat. "I'm not borrowing the only one of those that exists. She'd know. From four days off, she'd know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:125:The gate clerk leaned out of the lodge window with her list. "Four of you going out," she said. "I've had you down as going out since the second bell yesterday. Are you going out, or are you going to stand in my gateway all morning admiring the archivist?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:135:*Day one. Two. Both mounted, both in grey, a quarter-mile back on the road from the foot of the hill, keeping the same distance whether we walk or stop. Changed at the second waystation for two more, also grey, also mounted. Neither pair spoke to the other. They nodded.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:143:"Same," said Karis. "Except you've got the second pair changing horses and I haven't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:149:*Day two. Three. The two mounted from yesterday's change, and one on the coach with us from the second stage, a grey coat with a satchel on his knees. He took the seat with the widest view of the four of us, and kept it. He read a book. He didn't turn a page in an hour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:151:That afternoon, rattling along in the coach with the grey coat across from them pretending to read, Brom leaned across and spoke to Cael very low.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:187:Brom had come in behind him with two mugs of something hot from the kitchen. He set one down at Cael's elbow, read the page over his shoulder without asking, and sat down on the end of the bed, which complained.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:191:"Less than it should." Cael turned the mug on the table and thought about it again, because Brom's questions deserved a second try. "They're in plain sight. I've got every one of them on a page, and a fair idea of the bill, and I could tell you now where they'll stand at breakfast. That's not what frightens me." He looked at the page. "What would frighten me is a morning with nobody on the road behind us, and no reason I could find for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:207:"Seven." She came in and sat on the other end of the bed from Brom, who made room. "Then here's what I keep coming back to. They aren't hiding. That's a decision. And they aren't stopping, either, not even now that the ruling's public and there's nothing left to find. That's a second decision, and it costs money every month, and somebody has to put their name to it every month." She closed the notebook on her pencil. "The first one I understand. That second one has a name signed under it somewhere, every month. I'd like to read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:7:He looked up from his own sheet, which was a letter to nobody yet. For most of a week he had been trying to write a second letter to Vell, one that did not sound like a boy asking whether his first had arrived, and every version he tried came out sounding exactly like that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:15:The book in front of her was one of the founding compilations, a squat grey thing with boards gone soft at the corners. It had come back up the hill in the second cart, and Prynn had shelved it with her own hands three days before the term began. Karis had been working through its run since the first morning of the first week, and she had a method for it that Cael had watched long enough to know by heart. She read the table of contents, then the index, then every page in order from the first, and she copied out anything at all that used the old language, the language from before the schedule had a name for everybody, into the notebook with the brown ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:21:She did not answer him, only read the four lines under her finger again, slowly. Her lips moved on one word, and he was close enough to hear it, though barely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:39:He let his eyes go past her, down the long aisle between the cases, to the second-to-last case against the old wall. The traveller registers stood in their run on the third shelf, crosshatched spines in a row, and between two of them was the space the width of one volume. Nobody had closed it in the three weeks since. Nobody, he was sure now, ever would while Prynn was alive to stop them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:43:Outside, through the high window, he could see a slice of the gate and the end of the training hall. The man on the bench by the training hall door was there, as he was there every afternoon, with his hands on his knees and his hat on the bench beside him. He was one of five, and like the other four he did not trouble to hide; nobody had hidden since the ruling, and somewhere around the second week of the recess Cael had stopped flinching at it, which he counted as progress.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:61:"It's a mint word," Karis agreed. "It's a smith's word too, and an assayer's, which is the same word in an apron. Somebody brings you a piece of metal that claims to be silver. You don't take the stamp's word for it, or the merchant's. You put it to the test, and what it does under the test tells you what it is." She had her oldest notebook open, the one with the brown ink. "There's a clause in a founding-era compilation that uses the word about people. A charter clause, four lines long. It sets out how a chartered academy could once evaluate a practitioner for whom no registry classification existed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:85:Last term he had stood up in a room full of people and read their own schedule back to them, from the first page to the last, and he had won because he had not reached past the text by a single line. If he let himself start wanting this clause to be alive, he would end by reading it the way the Compact's clerks had read his own file for two years: hoping, and writing the hope down as though it were a finding, and never once noticing the difference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:91:"I mean it." He turned his cup on the wood. "If it can be killed, I'd rather it died in your notebook than in somebody else's courtroom with my name on the paper. Tell me what to read. I'll read it looking for the line that ends it, not the line that saves it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:95:"That's the only kind of help that's any use to me," she said. "Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:105:On the first morning back she had put him on the defensive floor with Hobb at the north mark and watched him run the framework at panel speed, four passes, without a word. On the second morning she broke the count on the third pass, as she always broke it, and said, "Pivot." On the third morning she did not say it. He had spent the whole of the recess on that pivot: on the frozen yard behind the residence wing, on the cold boards of his own room, in the dark before the first bell, turning on the ball of the foot again and again and feeling for the exact place where the weight came over late. A tenth of a beat was not much. It was the difference between a slip that took him out of a strike and a slip that took him half out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:115:Then came the Pressure-adjacent, which he did not use. After it came the Iron-adjacent read, Brom's, which laid mass and direction on the skin of his forearms a pace and a half out and never once told him what anybody meant to do, and the Compression-adjacent, which took a push and sent it down through his bones into the floor. Last and newest was the Ember-adjacent, which let go of fire at a single point and cost him the same whatever size he asked for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:123:*Exhibition bout, full sanction. Faculty supervised, on the record. Presiding: Instructor Wray. Second seat: Assessor Quenna. Eight exchanges on the instructor's count, standings rules. Week twenty, first bell, the training hall.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:127:"I asked for week ten. She said I wasn't ready." Edran took the sheet back and folded it along its crease. "I asked for week fifteen. She said neither of us was." He looked out at the floor, where a pair of second-years were running the Shield drill with a great deal of effort and very little Shield. "She was right the first time. I don't know yet about the second."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:141:"He'll be slower to reach you," said Hobb, and then thought about it some more, while Edran built a second shell and let it go in a spray of pale glitter. "Or he won't. I don't know which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:143:It was the most Cael had ever heard Hobb say about another fighter. He wrote it down that night under Edran's name, with the date, and drew a question mark in the margin beside it. Then he drew a second one, because one did not seem enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:151:She did not use the word in the letters. She cited the clause by its compilation, its volume and its page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:153:"If they want to know what it says," she said, "they can go and read it. Anybody who can't be bothered to read four lines isn't somebody whose answer I want."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:165:Karis wrote it. It took her most of an evening, because Prynn sent the first request back across the room with a single line drawn under the word *everything* and nothing else on it at all. The second request named eleven titles. Prynn read it, initialed it, and fetched seven of them. She came back to the table with a slip that accounted for the other four: two had never been bought, one had been lent to a provost in the year of the great flood and not returned, and one had gone to a binder in the regional town to be resewn and was not yet back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:167:Karis read the slip and looked up at Prynn, and Prynn looked back at her and said nothing whatever, and went back to her desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:171:There were three places a repealed clause would have had to go. The first was the directive's own repeal schedule, the long numbered list at its back of everything it struck by name, and the second was the registry's consolidated list of repeals, which some patient clerk had drawn up twenty years later out of everything struck by anybody. The third was the guild digest, in which the member guilds had printed, with some bad temper, every clause they had ever lost. Karis gave him the guild digest because it was the worst printed of the three and the likeliest to hide something in small type. He read it with a ruler under each line, as Vell had once taught him to read a ledger, every entry in order and none of them softer than any other. It took him nine evenings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:175:The replies began coming up the hill in week seven. The first, from a house on the coast, said in a page and a half that it held no such compilation and wished her well in her studies. The second, from an academy in the north, said the clause was obsolete and that it knew of no academy that had used it in living memory, and Karis wrote *obsolete is not repealed* in the margin in pencil, very small. The third and the fourth said nothing useful at some length. The fifth came from a provost's secretary, and Karis read it aloud at supper because it was beautifully written and said nothing at all, and Lira laughed until she had to put her spoon down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:183:Karis read it at the long table. Then she read it again. She leaned back until the chair creaked and studied the dark beams overhead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:203:Karis read it, and went white, and said nothing. Then she fetched the founding compilation and laid the two side by side, and they sat over them until the bell. The struck clause was not theirs. It was the one above it, a cousin that dealt with registry clerks and not with academies, and had borrowed half its wording. Somebody with the directive's pen had read that far, had struck the cousin by name, and had gone no further.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:209:The assay clause was in the founding text, and in the first consolidation word for word. In the second consolidation, printed after the directive, it was gone. There was no strike in its place and no note and no number. It was simply not there, the way a coat stops hanging on a hook when somebody takes it down one autumn and forgets to put it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:217:"It was orphaned. Everybody stopped using it, so the second consolidation left it out. But nobody who had the power to kill it ever did." She laid her palm flat on the second consolidation. "There's a gap where it used to stand, and not one line anywhere that put it there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:231:"You did. And I told you a gap isn't anything you can put a house on." Karis picked her pen up again and drew a clean sheet toward her. "This isn't a gap. It's a clause in force, in a founding schedule, that a chartered academy can use if its own charter carries the old text forward under one of the standard articles. It's a floor." She dipped the pen. "So now I tell somebody. I tell a registrar in a house chartered a hundred and forty-one years ago, in the old language, from its first article to its last, who has spent six weeks trying his hardest to prove me wrong and has failed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:3:The training hall had been swept twice before the first bell, once by the second-years whose turn it was and once by Hobb, who had not been asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:15:He stood with his weight low and easy, the off arm carried a little forward, close in along the line of his body, and he was not building anything yet. He looked up as Cael crossed the boards, and there was nothing in his face but attention.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:19:"Exhibition bout," she said, and her voice went to the back of the hall without her raising it. "Under full sanction, before the faculty, on the record, as both parties asked. Eight exchanges on my count. Standings rules. Each exchange goes to the cleaner touch, or I call it even. Any structure on the body is lawful. Any fragment or declaration the record already carries is lawful. Nothing to the head, nothing to the joints, nothing after my word." She looked at Edran, and then at Cael. "Either of you may stop it. I may stop it. If I stop it, it's stopped, and nobody argues with me afterward in the yard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:29:*Three bursts free. The fourth bills the hip in the morning. The read works and tells me nothing about what he means. No Ember. Compression only if he puts a push in me I can't get out of.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:35:Across the boards Edran rolled his shoulders once and settled. Cael ran the gaze over him from the floor up, the way he would have run it over a stranger in the Ironyard: feet a little wider than they had been in the autumn, the weight even across both, the knees soft. The breath was high and slow. The off arm hung forward and close, the hand open, the forearm turned a little out so that its edge faced the line between them. The lead arm was the same as ever. Every layer agreed with every other, and none of them told him anything he had not seen from the rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:47:That was the first thing. The old Edran had come off his mark almost at once, faster than the textbook distance, with a shell already bright along his leading forearm, driving it out to be struck. This one came a step and then let Cael come to him, with the structure forming on the lead arm only as Cael closed: a hard pale shell like ice skinning over a trough, there and finished in the space of a blink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:49:Cael read it. The read laid it on the skin of his forearms a pace and a half out, the weight of the shell and the line it was set along, a fraction before his eyes had it. The gaze had the rest: Edran's breath, held high; his rear foot, flat; the off arm close in, doing nothing. Everything agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:55:A second shell had come up on the off arm, which had been carried forward and close in exactly so that it could, and it was already set across the space Cael had moved into. He felt it on the read a breath too late to stop. He turned his shoulder and took the shell on the outside of his forearm, where it burst and stung, and Edran's lead hand came round inside it with the edge of the forearm turned out and laid itself along his ribs. It barely touched, but it was clean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:61:Cael went back to his mark with his forearm humming and the gaze running over what had happened, looking for the mistake. He could not find one. He had read the shell right, the burst right, and the bare arm right, and moved at the right speed. And the off arm had been there anyway, because it had never been out of place to begin with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:65:This time he did not go in. He let Edran come to him, and stayed at the edge of the read, and watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:69:He tried changing his own timing. He went in early on the third shell, before it burst, to catch the off arm on its way. The off arm was not on its way anywhere. It had never left. He tried going in late, after the spray, when the off arm had closed and might be committed, and found the lead already building again behind it. Edran did not hurry. He did not need to. He gave Cael the floor, a step at a time, and took it back a step at a time, and kept his breath high and slow the whole while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:71:Every read he had was correct. Every read arrived at the right place half a beat after Edran did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:73:He understood it in the middle of a slip, with a shell bursting off his shoulder and the spray stinging the side of his neck. Edran had given up reach. With the off arm carried forward and close, it could not go out far to meet anything. But it was always nearly in place already. When a shell burst on the lead, the off arm did not have to come from somewhere; it only had to close the last finger's width. That was what the hand of reach had bought, and it was exactly the half-beat Cael's eyes needed. Edran had not hidden his seam. He had moved his recovery to the place where Cael's reading ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:75:A correct read that came too late, Cael thought, was a wrong read, only more expensive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:83:Reading the shell would not do it. The shell told him where Edran was, and Edran's whole new stance was built to be somewhere else half a beat later. He had to read something that moved before the shell did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:87:Edran came on, and Cael let him, and this time he did not watch the lead. He let the lead arm go to the edge of the gaze, where it could be weather. He put his attention on the off arm, carried forward and close, and on the shoulder above it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:89:The read gave it to him on the second shell. A fraction before the off arm closed its last finger's width, the weight came off the rear foot and into the off shoulder, and the read laid that shift on the skin of his forearms like a hand pressing. It was small, and it was early. It came before the shell burst, before anything Cael's eyes could see, and it told him the off arm was about to move across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:105:Edran came on as before, and Cael waited for the shift, and the read gave it to him: weight off the rear foot, into the off shoulder, the off arm about to move across. He went with it, left and low, to the side it was leaving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:109:Edran had put the weight into the shoulder and held it there and built his next shell on the lead instead, the old way, and Cael came out of his slip into exactly the place the lead shell was waiting. He got his forearm up. The shell burst on it with a crack like a branch breaking under snow, and the off arm, which had been loaded and still, came across now, late on purpose, and laid its edge along his flank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:115:Edran had learned the read in one exchange. He had felt where Cael came through and given him the same shift with nothing behind it, and Cael had believed it, because it was true weight, honestly placed. The read never lied about weight. It only ever lied about what weight was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:121:It went long. Edran pressed, and Cael gave ground slipping, and twice the shift came and twice he waited, and twice it was nothing. On the third the off arm moved. It moved fast, but Cael was already turning. The framework took him round on the second figure, the turn Wray had broken his count on all term and that he had spent the whole recess mending on frozen stone, and it brought his weight over on time, not a tenth late. He came out of the pivot square to Edran's open side with his feet under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:123:The second burst was short and almost easy. He had his palm on Edran's breastbone and the landing beat paid before the off arm had finished crossing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:133:Neither of them waited. Edran came off his mark with a shell already up, faster than he had come all morning, and Cael understood that Edran meant to take the exchange before the read could settle. He came in close and stayed close, building and shedding on both arms so fast that the read was a smear of weight and the gaze could barely keep up. Cael slipped once, and again, and on the third shell he felt the shift and saw the off arm commit, and he went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:137:But Edran had committed honestly this time, and he was quick. As Cael came out of the burst on the open side, Edran was already turning into him, shedding the lead shell in a spray that went into Cael's face and building the next on the off arm as it came round. Cael's palm went to the breastbone. Edran's structure went to Cael's chest. The spray from the burst and the touch of the palm arrived together, so close together that Cael himself could not have said which was first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:165:He went to the read and the gaze and the framework, slipping on his own feet and taking shells on his forearms. He waited for the shift, and on the second true one he went in on foot, without a burst, using the pivot, aiming to arrive on the open side by turning instead of flying. It was slower. It was half a beat slower, which was exactly the half-beat Edran had bought with his reach. He arrived on the open side, and Edran's third structure of the sequence was standing there on the off arm, whole, thick and clean from the elbow to the wrist, without any thin place or cold sliver on its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:181:Edran came on. He knew it as well as Cael did. He came on low and patient and let the shells come up and shed, lead and off, lead and off, and waited for the fourth burst to come. If Cael had been in his place, he would have been doing exactly the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:185:The first was a feint. He felt it, and held, and the off arm stayed. The second was a feint too, and he held again. On the third, the weight went into the off shoulder and the arm began to move, and Cael held half a breath and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:197:That one had been the noise of people who had seen something happen that they had not expected. This one was the noise of people who had watched two fighters do a thing all the way through, and knew that it had been close, and could not agree how close. Half the north bench was shouting about the sixth exchange. The tout had climbed down from the bench and was sitting on it with his head in his hands, which Cael took to mean he had laid his crusts badly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:227:He had won. He knew that. Wray had called it, and Quenna had written it, and two hundred people had seen it, and nobody could take it off the record now. He also knew how close it had been. He had won one of the eight exchanges on a call that could have gone the other way. He had won another on a burst he had promised himself he would not spend, which had landed half an inch short of where he put it. And he had lost a third to a man who had learned his best read in one exchange and handed it back to him empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:231:The first way was easy and true in every word. *Edran, exhibition, week twenty. Won four to three in the eighth exchange. Found the recovery in the off arm in the third; corrected in the fifth; pivot on time.* Every word of that was so, and it would read beautifully a year from now, and it would be a lie by omission. A lie by omission was still a lie, and he had built his whole life since Denvash on not telling any.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:235:*Edran. Week twenty. Full sanction, Wray presiding, Quenna second seat. Eight exchanges.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:239:*What he built: he gave up a hand of reach to carry the off arm forward and close, so that his recovery is already in place when the lead bursts. Every read I had arrived correct and half a beat late. A correct read that comes too late is a wrong read that costs more.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:241:*Correction (found in the third, broken in the fourth, mended in the fifth): don't read the shell, read the off arm's shift. Then wait to see whether the arm follows, because he'll feed me the shift empty. That costs the time I was trying to save. It's still the only read that worked.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:247:He read it back once. It was not flattering. He found he did not mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:249:Lira knocked and came in without waiting, as she did. She had a cloth folded in her hand, wet from the pump and cold enough that he could feel it across the room. She held it out. When he took it, she leaned over his shoulder and read the page in the binder without asking, all the way down to the last line, and straightened up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:3:The books were only half of those eleven weeks. All the while the consolidations lay open down one side of the long table, the second hunt ran beside them by coach, four days each way, and it had its own surprises.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:15:There were five groups. The first was about the founding text and Prynn's copies of it. The second was about the consolidations, and it went through them one by one, by title, and asked of each of them a question Karis had not yet thought to ask. The third was about the three repeal lists. The fourth was headed *The candidate*, and the fifth was headed *Matters I may have missed*, and contained only one question, which was whether there were any.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:27:He read the fourth group through twice before he picked up a pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:43:He read it back, and pushed the sheet across to Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:45:She read it once, and looked at him over the top of it for a long moment without saying anything, and then folded it into her answers with the rest. When the bundle went down the hill on the Wednesday coach, that line was in it, in his own hand, on its way to a man he had never met, and he found he did not mind at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:51:By then there were a great many letters, because Bracken did not wait for one answer before he wrote the next: he wrote by every coach, sometimes two letters in one packet, numbered on the outside so that Karis would read them in the order he had written them and not the order the post delivered them. Karis kept them in a bundle tied with grey string, which she had cut from the end of one of the delegation's boxes when they came back in the autumn and had been saving for something worth tying. The bundle was as thick as two fingers now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:53:The fifteenth was the thickest letter yet. Karis opened it at the long table after supper, with Lira on the bench by the door and Brom at the end of the table with his pocket book, and Cael across from her waiting to be handed his part. She read the first page. She turned to the second. She read that too, and the third, and then she went back to the first and read the whole thing again, much more slowly, with her finger moving down the margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:65:"What does it mean?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:67:"I don't know." Karis said it as if it hurt. "I never read it properly. I skipped it, because it wasn't where a repeal would be." She laid the page down. "He's walked through a door in my own house that I walked past for eleven weeks. I'm going to read it tonight, every word, and I'm going to be cross the whole time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:93:Karis answered the fifteenth letter that night, and it was the shortest letter she wrote all term. Cael watched her draft it three times. The first draft ran to a page and explained, at some length, the road she had taken and where it had crossed his. The second ran to half a page and thanked him. She tore both of them across and fed the halves to the stove at the far end of the archive, which Prynn permitted only for drafts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:95:The third draft was one line, and she let him read it before she sealed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:101:"He didn't ask me anything." Karis pressed the seal down with her thumb. "When somebody sends you a finding without a single question in it, the only polite thing to do is not to make them read a page of yours." She held the letter up to the lamp and looked at it. "Besides, I want him to know I can be short. He's going to be reading my letters for years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:"What does it say?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:117:Cael read it, and it was very short. He did not understand all of it, and what he did understand was dry and exact and careful in the way the best of Karis's sentences were careful, never claiming one word more than it could carry. At the foot was a signature he could not read, and under it a line in another hand, *Seen. Withrow.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:121:Naveth picked it up and read it again from the beginning. He had read it before Cael came in, Cael was sure; the clerk had said so. He read it now with his spectacles pushed up onto his forehead and the paper held a little away, as a man reads a letter he already knows and wants to see once more. His face, which Cael had watched break once on a witness floor and then mend, did something small and complicated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:137:He had known for weeks which way it would go, and if he was honest he had known it since the night in the archive when Karis laid her pen down square to the table. But knowing a thing and deciding it were different, and he had been careful all term to let them stay different until there was something on paper. Now there was. He stopped at the turn of the stair by the window, where he had read so many things that touched his own name, and looked down the hill at the road going away east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:153:"He's a registrar," said Karis. "Assuming is what they do between filings. He also said he'd prefer all four of us at the gate on the same morning, because it'd save him a second walk to the lodge." She had her narrow brown notebook open, the watcher log, beside her bowl. "I've got a research standing to argue for. Archive access, terms written down. A library committee with a charter, he says, which means the terms can be read, and anything that can be read can be argued with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:157:Lira did not say what hers was. They all knew. Her papers would travel south ahead of her, the old ones from Fenmark and the new one from this hill. The new one was the best thing Greyvane had been able to give her, and Greyvane had never had the weight to make anybody act on it: a flag asking the registry to look at her tier again, signed by a provost who had watched her put Iron-equivalent fighters on the floor for two whole terms. It was the best Greyvane could do. Everybody at the table knew it was not enough. Her formal standing still said *Copper, Rank Two*, and a ladder read off formal standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:159:Lira had wanted, for as long as Cael had known her, one thing that nobody had ever been willing to give her. It was not a rank, though she would take the rank. It was not a crown or a purse or a better room. It was to stand in front of the instrument that had read her at fourteen and set her down as a Copper practitioner, and to make it look again. She had said so once, on a road, in the dark, and never again, and he had not forgotten it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:161:"There's a ladder," said Lira, as if she had heard him think it. "Brom asked. A real one, with brackets, and boards up in a yard where everybody can read them, and a season." Her eyes went very bright and very hard. "Here they gave me two bouts in a closed yard and wrote me down as a flag. There, they'll have to watch me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:179:Through weeks fifteen to nineteen he watched it from the rail whenever he could, and it did not get any easier to read, because Edran had rebuilt himself over the recess and through the whole of the term, an hour at a time, with Wray standing over him, and the man at the far end of the hall was not the one Cael had beaten in the formal yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:193:"I can't fight what I can't read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:195:"You've been fighting things you can't read since you were fourteen." Lira did not soften it. "You read faster than anybody I've ever met. That's yours, and it's real. But it's a habit as well, and a habit is a thing the other man can learn about you." She came at him again, without warning, and he slipped her, and she stopped. "There. You read me, and you went. Edran's watched you do that more than anyone alive except me. He's had a whole term to think about what to do with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:199:"Count the half-second," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:5:*Persons on foot, two coppers. Persons with a pack beast, five. Handcart, laden, four. Handcart, empty, two.* Then a line for drovers, charged by the head, and a line for wagons, charged by the axle. Below those were three he had to read twice. *Persons bearing goods for the customs house, no charge on the house's tally. Persons of the river guilds, by their lodge's arrangement. Academy enrollees in the first week of any term, on presentation of the gate list, one copper.* The last had been painted in later than the rest, in a whiter white, and its price had been painted over at least twice. Every line on that board, Cael thought, was the healed-over place where somebody had won an argument with the toll keeper's grandfather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:19:Below them the Ost went by wide and slow and brown. A barge was working up against it, sitting low with sacks under a tarred cloth, two men leaning on the long sweeps and a boy at the tiller, the three of them bent as though they were shoving the whole river uphill. Another came down the other way easily, sweeps shipped, and its master stood on the cabin roof and put a horn to his mouth and blew three short notes. From somewhere on the near bank a second horn answered with two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:25:Along the near bank the town went on as far as he could see each way, warehouses shouldering down to the water. He counted six long sheds with their doors open on the river and a seventh going up in raw yellow timber. A public wharf had a wooden crane on it with four men walking its wheel, and below that a second, smaller wharf stood behind a fence with a company's mark on its gate. Between the bridge foot and the public wharf, across the only street that ran down to the water, a building of good grey stone sat with its own steps and its own dock and a flag over its door. Nothing could come off a boat and into Ostrand, Cael saw, without passing under that flag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:31:"I don't know yet." Karis said it the way she said things she meant to fix. "But Lira's flag is going into a registry somewhere, and when it comes out it'll come out of one of those two doors. I want to know which door before it does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:41:He had been keeping the count of days since the gate at Greyvane, every evening, in the margin of the observation notebook. It was five months and nine days, tonight, since a magistrate had stood up in a crowded hall and read him a ruling. He was fifteen years old. He had added the months up twice on the road against Hesk's last letter, because he no longer trusted any number that only he was keeping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:77:He found the page without hunting for it. He read it off fast and flat, in the voice of a man who had read sixty names since the first bell and could hear the next cart's wheels already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:79:"Dellenmoor. Standard. Research standing pending; the library committee writes to you. Brom. Standard. Ninth day, first bell, east hall; the Blade cohort wants a look at you. Lira. Transfer, re-certification. Copper bracket. You register for the ladder by the fourteenth or you start the season on the bottom rung and climb from there." He licked a finger and turned the page. "Cael. Assay-provision enrollment. Straight to the registrar. Assessment wing, ground floor, the door with the brass plate. He's cleared the whole morning, which he does not do, so go now and look at the view later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:83:"Rooms, second quadrangle. The porter's got your keys. The assessment wing has a wall of its own; you'll know it. Don't cross the Crown yard when there's a bout on; they'll shout at you, and I'll be glad they did." He was already reaching for the next carter's slip. "Welcome to Halcenvane. You there, with the casks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:87:Cael had braced himself for it since the bridge. The only other gate in his life that had kept a list with his name on it had nearly stopped him dead on its threshold with something too large to have a name. He waited for that to happen again, and it did not. He walked through between the folded leaves and out the other side, and the only thing he felt was a kind of quiet click, like a latch going home in a door that had always been meant to close. Four people, four lines in a stranger's book, and four roads already decided in ink by people who had never seen their faces. Nobody at this gate had needed to argue for any of it. The arguing had been done somewhere else, months ago, on paper, and here was only the result.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:The other eight stood under linen dust sheets down the far end of the room, in two rows, square and pale, like a second set of applicants who had come early and been told to wait. Cael stood in the line for the fourth desk and looked at them for a long time. The hall ran a hundred feet under a coffered ceiling, lit down its whole west wall by tall windows, on an oak floor laid for this room and no other. Somebody had sized it for an academy three times the one that was using it this morning. Somebody had looked at Halcenvane as it was, and built a room for Halcenvane as it meant to become, and paid for it, and then sat down to wait for the school to grow into its own furniture.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:115:Cael could not hear all of it from the bench, but he could see it, and he could read Lira standing at an official table with her arms folded better than he could read most people's faces. Her clerk was a grey-haired woman with spectacles on a cord. Lira's file had come up from Greyvane ahead of her: the old Fenmark record, the acknowledgment of standing she had signed before any of them knew her, and, pinned at the back, the sheet Greyvane had given her. It was the flag asking the registry to look at her tier again, under Greyvane's seal, the provost's whole two terms of watching folded into one line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:117:The clerk read the flag through. She gave it a single nod, wrote two words beside it, and set it on a pile by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:127:They went on for a while longer, the clerk explaining and Lira not arguing, which was worse. *Only an Arbiter evaluation can change the tier*, the clerk said at one point, clearly enough for Cael to hear it across the room, and *I don't like the pace of it any more than you do, dear*, and *register early; the people who matter read the boards.* And then Lira unfolded her arms, put both hands flat on the edge of the desk, and said something that made the clerk look up at her properly for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:129:The clerk thought about it. Then she dipped her pen and wrote a line at the foot of Lira's sheet, and turned it round so Lira could read it, and Lira read it and nodded, and signed under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:145:"You've read the letters," he said. "All of them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:*Enrollment record. Halcenvane Academy, chartered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:163:He read it once. Bracken took it back and set it face down on the desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:167:In the first stack lay the clause, copied out of three founding-era compilations, every copy certified, every one tagged with where its book stood and which clerk had sworn to it. Bracken read out each mark. Cael, because the index was in his bag, took it out and found each one in Prynn's small upright hand and read her cross-reference back. Two of the compilations were ones Karis had worked from. The third Greyvane had never owned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:169:On the second certified copy, Cael stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:"The copying clerk's slip," he said. "He took the shelf-mark off the first printing and the certificate off the second, because they stand side by side. The text's the same in both. I checked that myself. But the sheet says two things at once, and anybody who wanted to could pull on that thread." He set the sheet aside on a pile of its own. "It'll be redone this week. That's what I asked you here for. Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:177:Bracken called the second stack the negative case. In it were the three lists of repeals, and the consolidations, and a table ruled in his own hand, list against list and page against page, with the clause missing from every column. Under the table he had written two sentences. The first said what the absence showed. The second said, just as plainly, what it did not show, and why nobody should read it as showing that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:179:"Most people would leave the second sentence out," said Bracken. "It looks like weakness. It's the strongest line in the file." He tapped it. "Never let a document," he said, "claim more than it proves. If it claims one line more, that one line is where it gets attacked. Then people stop reading, and the whole file goes into the fire with its true lines and its false one together."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:183:"She's seventeen, your Dellenmoor. I asked her outright, after my forty-one came back answered better than I'd asked them. I didn't believe the first reply." Bracken squared the second stack. "Third."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:189:"Your floor and your difficulty, both in one binding," said Bracken. "Your hearing settled that what people do and what is written are two different things, and the written one wins. That's your floor. This clause is written, and all that ever happened to it was people stopping. And it puts your name at the top of a public record that anybody between here and the coast can send for at six silver marks. That's permanent. Both halves of it are permanent." He looked at Cael over the boards. "You knew that when you argued it, I suppose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:197:He turned the enrollment record face up and slid it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:199:Cael read it a second time, and then a third, and knew he was doing it, and knew why. He had done it once before. That had been at a narrow table at Greyvane, with Quenna on one side of him and a cold cup at his elbow, over a form that said *unclassified observer*. He had read it three times then not to understand it, but to make himself agree to sign a thing that was not him. The form had held. Every word on it had been true. Not one of them had been about him, and everybody at that table had known it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:209:"Two signatures," he said. "The first is for the standing. The second is for the obligation, and I want it on its own line, in its own stroke. A man who signs both with one movement can say later he only read the half he liked." He watched Cael's hand. "Somebody will come up that road one day to read this with a grudge. Inside two years, I'd guess. Keep that in the same pocket as the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:Cael signed twice, in the square hand he kept for the binder. He watched the second signature go dull as the ink dried. Something rose in his chest and his eyes stung, all at once and without asking him. He turned his head and looked very hard at the window, which showed a slice of pale wall and a gutter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:225:"Twelve years I've kept these rolls," he said. "In twelve years, every record I've filed has been true because nobody ever had cause to look at it closely. Yours had to be true *because* I did the work properly. One day somebody will be paid to hunt for the flaw in it." He looked up. "Do you know how long I've waited for a file like that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:231:It ran along the edge of the bluff behind the second quadrangle, chest-high, the same pale stone as everything else, and beyond it the ground fell away in rough terraces to the water. Ostrand lay across the river with the late light going gold along its roofs. Down on the wharves somebody was lighting lamps, one after another, and the lamps ran out along the waterfront like a line of stitches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:255:"Two terms of putting Iron-equivalents on the floor. An academy's seal on a flag. A queue that's answered that woman three times since the spring before last." She ate the bread. "And they read the number on my certificate, and they stop reading. They always have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:277:*Karis found it and tried for eleven weeks to kill it, and it wouldn't die. Bracken checked it from the other end because he wanted to, not because anyone asked. A counsel I'll never meet put a seal on it, and Naveth read the seal twice and was glad for me in a way that cost him. Every form anybody ever got me through before was true in its words and false about me. This one is true about me. It took a lot of people to make one sentence.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:283:*What it costs: a panel every semester, a room, a full evaluation each time, and a chair in that room for the Compact whenever it wants one. Whatever I show Gault first becomes the baseline, and the baseline doesn't move. For as long as I'm here. Nobody has ever told me a price in advance before, in writing, and let me read it twice before I signed.*

