editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:107:He watched her go with the sheaf in his hands, and saw he had had the gap the wrong way round all term. Prynn was not failing to close it. She was keeping it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:19:"Exhibition bout," she said, and her voice went to the back of the hall without her raising it. "Under full sanction, before the faculty, on the record, as both parties asked. Eight exchanges on my count. Standings rules. Each exchange goes to the cleaner touch, or I call it even. Any structure on the body is lawful. Any fragment or declaration the record already carries is lawful. Nothing to the head, nothing to the joints, nothing after my word." She looked at Edran, and then at Cael. "Either of you may stop it. I may stop it. If I stop it, it's stopped, and nobody argues with me afterward in the yard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:97:Lira knocked her shoulder against his, and Brom took out his small pocket book and wrote something in it. When Cael raised his eyebrows, Brom turned the page round. It said *Week three. K. found a word. We're trying to kill it.* Under it was a single tally mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:5:She was big and tired and kind, with flour worked into the creases of her knuckles, and she ladled stew as if every bowl were a small argument she meant to win. He had thanked her every time, and on the thirteenth evening she leaned toward him over the hatch with the ladle still in her hand and lowered her voice, so that the line behind him would not hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:35:Past that they went wide. He had no Path at all. He had a secret one. He was a Gold in disguise. He was the Compact's kept boy. He had fought a Warden bare-handed and put him on the floor, which had never happened anywhere, and which he could not trace to anything at all. He had been put out of two academies. He was twelve. He was twenty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:43:*At Greyvane the question of what I was had no answer, and so it stayed open. That was hard to live in, but an open door can still be walked through later, by somebody carrying proof.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:13:The assessment wing had its own wall and its own gate, and through an open door on the ground floor he counted pieces of apparatus standing along a wall under cloths. There were eleven, and he could guess the purpose of four. The library was the heavy-roofed building he had picked out from the bridge, and an accession board inside its door claimed forty-one thousand volumes in brass numerals. Karis, when he told her that night, said that accession boards counted everything a library had ever been given, including the things it had since burned for being damp, and that she would believe thirty-five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:79:He rebuilt it that evening in carrel eleven, because Karis had a door that shut and a lamp that did not smoke, and because he did not want to do it alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:95:*Sampled.* These were people he would look at properly, but only at set times, one session in four, with the date written beside every entry, so that he would always know how old a thing was before he leaned on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:99:His pen did not want to write the third tier, because to put it down was to admit, on paper, that the hill held four hundred people he had already chosen not to see properly. He made the pen do it. Then he boxed the reason, so that he would have to look at it every time he opened the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:137:He kept the Crown yard back until the sixth morning on purpose, because he wanted the whole bluff understood first, so that when he finally stood in the middle he would know what it was the middle of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:143:That had been meant. Somebody had dug a hole and put the fighting at the bottom of it, so that a thousand people could sit above and look down on two. He stood at the bottom of it in the grey light and felt it on the back of his neck, the way he had felt the gaze of a full gallery at Greyvane, though there was nobody here at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:237:*Eleven days. Why they took me: not finished. But I know what they spend on, and that's half of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:241:*A circuit that pays coin has to tell you the price before the bout. You know what you're fighting for, and so does everybody watching. A ladder that pays in standing never has to say. It just moves you up or down a wall, and lets you work out afterward what it cost.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:43:He wrote to Hesk on the Tuesday night. It was a short letter, shorter than the last, because he had learned that Hesk read the short ones more than once. He said where they were going, and why, and that there was a registrar at the other end who had asked how to spell Hesk's name and had got it in capitals. He said the four of them were well. He did not say anything about the bout. He thought about it, and found what he wanted to write was *I won by one exchange and maybe none*, and he could not see a way to put that to Hesk that would not sound like asking to be told it was all right. He left it out. Hesk would hear it from somebody, or he would not, and either way it would keep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:65:She went back up the rail and out by the side door, and that was all the goodbye there was. He stood on her stone a while longer and found it had been exactly enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:133:Cael counted them from the first morning, because counting was what he did with his hands when there was nothing else for them, and because a thing counted was a thing he could stop being afraid of. He did not have to look hard. Nobody was hiding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:161:"If a man hides while he watches you, he wants to learn something," he said. "He wants you to forget he's there, so that you'll do whatever you'd have done anyway, and he can write it down. A watcher who doesn't hide wants the opposite." He kept his voice under the noise of the wheels. "He wants you to know. He wants it to change what you do. He's not collecting anything. He's trying to make something happen in you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:17:It was old stone and very long: five spans on four piers, each pier with a pointed cutwater shouldering into the current. Halfway across, at the crown of the middle span, Lira stopped at the parapet without saying why. The other three stopped with her, because that was what they did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:33:He had stopped trying, in the last week, to work it out from the rail. Lira had been right. He had been trying to fight the man he had beaten, and that man was gone. The one at the far mark had spent a whole term taking himself apart and putting himself back together on purpose, an hour at a time, and hating every hour that he needed it, and the only way to find out what he had built was to stand in front of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:175:Cael went back to his mark. His forearms ached from wrist to elbow. His breath was short and his hip was warm. There was one exchange left, and he had won three of them and been given one of those three, and Edran had won three outright. He stood at the mark and looked at the man at the other end of the boards. He knew he could not get to the open side in time on his feet, and that he had one burst left before the bill came due. He knew too that he was going to spend it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:239:*What he built: he gave up a hand of reach to carry the off arm forward and close, so that his recovery is already in place when the lead bursts. Every read I had arrived correct and half a beat late. A correct read that comes too late is a wrong read that costs more.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:259:She shut it behind her. He sat with the cold cloth on his hip and the binder open in front of him, and thought she was right, and that it was the best thing anybody had said to him all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:39:He wrote *Yes.* Then he wrote the name of the category, *unclassified observer*, and the term he had been enrolled under it, and that the form had been defensible on its face and had been challenged by the Compact and held. And then, because a file that left out the uncomfortable part was only half a file, he wrote one more line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:129:"It is the right house," said Naveth. "I want you to hear me say that, so that you never wonder afterward whether I said it through my teeth. Greyvane took you because it was the only door open, and because two people in it were stubborn. That was enough to get you through a winter. It is not enough to carry you through what comes after." He took his spectacles down off his forehead and folded them. "Halcenvane has a ladder and a library and counsel of its own. More to the point, it has something to prove, and it means to prove it with you. That isn't a kindness. You have had kindness. You need a house that wants you for a reason it can write down."
