+- current: `He's going to be reading my letters for years."`
+
+Every paragraph in all seven current chapters now has an even count of straight quotation marks.
+
+### Priority 2 — say it once
+
+**Assay-clause braid: RESOLVED.** The new end of chapter 1 explicitly separates the two tracks: “From that week there were two hunts on the long table instead of one. One was the books … The other was a stranger four days down the road.” Chapter 2 now opens, “The books were only half of those eleven weeks … the second hunt ran beside them by coach,” so the return to week eleven reads as the correspondence track running inside the already-established investigation rather than a reset.
+
+The duplicated proof was cut. The long comparison restatement became one sentence (“every clause in the old text was matched to whatever stood in its place in the new”), and Karis's conclusion is now limited to what the documents prove: “It was orphaned. Everybody stopped using it … But nobody who had the power to kill it ever did.” The replacement space supplies a genuine obstacle rather than another explanation: in week twelve Cael finds a similarly worded clause struck by name, Karis verifies that it is the registry-clerk cousin, and boxes its number for the future challenge.
+
+The transitional article is now only a plant. The pre-repair inference—“it presupposes them … You can't make provision for people enrolled under a clause you've repealed”—is gone. Current text says Bracken copied the article without comment, underlined two sentences, and Karis answers Brom's “What does it mean?” with “I don't know.” This preserves Ilsev's later protected finding for chapter 18.
+
+All required pieces remain: nine inquiries; Bracken's independent backward route; the fifteenth letter with no questions; Karis's one-line reply; counsel's seal; Naveth's reaction.
+
+**Chapter 4 farewells: RESOLVED.** Quenna's explanatory after-passage is reduced from four reiterative paragraphs to the one necessary inference and the consequence: “So she had spent one sentence and kept the paragraph. He did not go after her.” Prynn's long interpretation is now two short sentences: “Prynn was not failing to close it. She was keeping it.” Naveth, Wray, Quenna, and Prynn all remain, as do the notice, re-measure instruction, sleeve touch, index, and shelf-gap promise.
+
